decisions:
  - id: langchain4j_adapter_boundary
    summary: LangChain4j stays behind `adapters/langchain4j`; core and application stay provider-free
  - id: deterministic_approval_boundary
    summary: model output may propose or repair but validation, scoring, promotion and rollback are deterministic
  - id: live_loop_policy_proposal
    summary: CHG-002 proposes OpenAI via LangChain4j official adapter, TOON mutation-contract envelopes, S-expression internal mutation IR, closed mutation operator enum with hill-climb and exploratory-leap search posture operators, Git diff realization, conceptual crossover deferral, local promoted Git refs plus SQLite metadata and hard-gated phenotype fitness scoring
  - id: actor_review_evidence_policy
    summary: reviewer actors produce evidence only; unavailable actor review is recorded and never treated as passed
  - id: tool_unavailable_integration_fallback
    summary: PR-less main merge remains allowed only under explicit user authorization, branch isolation, deterministic checks, actor or substituted human review and lead self-review

risks:
  - CHG-002 is not yet approved; implementation should not hard-code these policies until the user accepts or revises them
  - first live provider choice can affect dependency and credential handling, so provider construction must remain adapter-local
  - the S-expression mutation IR needs a small parser/canonicalizer before implementation can rely on it
  - conceptual crossover is deliberately deferred until evaluated parent evidence exists
  - `exploratory-leap` needs strict risk budgets so moonshot variants do not become broad unreviewable changes
  - benchmark budgets may be too crude until baseline and candidate benchmarks are captured in one comparable run
  - local promoted refs are auditable but may not satisfy future protected-branch governance
  - Gradle commands need Nix daemon access in this environment; sandboxed runs can fail even when project commands pass outside the sandbox

